---
const jobs = [
  ["run", "Noun: A run around the park was fun.", "Verb: I run around the park."],
  ["before", "Preposition: before the show", "Conjunction: before the show began"],
  ["yet", "Conjunction: tired, yet she worked", "Adverb: I haven’t finished yet."],
  ["those", "Determiner: those books", "Pronoun: Those are mine."],
  ["outside", "Adverb: waited outside", "Preposition: outside the shop"]
];
---

<div class="topic-sheet">
  <h1>Interjections - Expressing a reaction</h1>
  <p>
    An <strong>interjection</strong> is a short expression of a reaction or feeling:
    <strong>Oh! Wow! Hurray! Ouch! Alas!</strong> The punctuation changes the tone. “Oh!” can show surprise.
    “Oh.” can show a quieter realisation.
  </p>
  <div class="summary-stack">
    <section class="summary-block summary-peach">
      <h2>It is extra to the sentence</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Wow!</strong> The rainbow is bright. The sentence “The rainbow is bright” is still complete
        without <em>Wow!</em>
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="summary-block summary-pink">
      <h2>An exclamation mark does not make an interjection</h2>
      <p>
        In “<strong>Stop!</strong> The road is busy,” <em>Stop</em> is a command verb, not a feeling word.
        <strong>Ouch!</strong> is an interjection. <strong>Hurray!</strong> is an interjection.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <p class="try-it">
    <strong>Try it.</strong> Find the interjection in “Hurray! Our team won.” Is “Stop!” an interjection or a
    command in “Stop! The road is busy”?
  </p>
  <section class="summary-block summary-sand">
    <h2>A word’s job can change</h2>
    <table class="punct-table summary-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="th-feature" scope="col">Word</th>
          <th class="th-rule" scope="col">One job</th>
          <th class="th-example" scope="col">Another job</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          jobs.map(([word, one, another]) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{word}</th>
              <td>{one}</td>
              <td>{another}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</div>
